  "name": { "es": "Playa Sword", "en": "Sword Beach" },
  "description": {
    "es": "6 de junio de 1944. La 3.ª División británica desembarca en Queen Red y Queen White, los comandos de Lovat corren a Ouistreham y a los puentes del Orne, donde aguantan los paracaidistas, y por la tarde la 21.ª Panzer contraataca desde Caen hacia el hueco con Juno.",
    "en": "6 June 1944. The British 3rd Division lands on Queen Red and Queen White, Lovat's commandos race for Ouistreham and the Orne bridges held by the paratroopers, and in the afternoon 21st Panzer strikes back from Caen towards the gap with Juno."
  },
  "notes": "Made by Claude Code from history (Wikipedia's Sword Beach, Britannica, normandie-tourisme.fr, dday-overlord.com), not an official Memoir '44 scenario. The map runs from the Orne's east bank (Merville, Ranville, the Bois de Bavent) to Luc-sur-Mer at the edge of Juno, and from the sea to the north of Caen, squeezed north to south (about 400 m a hex across, 600 m a row). Strongpoints: WN10 Riva-Bella casino, WN14 Sole, WN16 Morris, WN17 Hillman, WN12 Daimler, WN18 Colleville-Plage, WN20 Cod at La Brèche, WN21 Trout at Lion, WN24 at Luc, the Douvres radar station and the Merville battery; the gun on the Périers ridge and the 21st Panzer guns by Épron are game licence. The Allies start at sea in three waves (DD tanks and AVREs with the East Yorks and South Lancs, then Lovat's commandos and 41 RM Commando, then the 185th Brigade, the Staffordshire Yeomanry and the SP field regiments) and 6th Airborne already holds the Orne bridges, Bénouville and Ranville; commandos and paratroopers are elite. The 21st Panzer starts far back round Caen, east of the Orne and towards the Juno gap, as it only struck at 16:00. Rulings to confirm with the player: Allied air superiority (2 air cards, 0 for the Axis), no fordable rivers, wire for the beach obstacles and minefields, 18 medals; Allied objectives Ouistreham, the casino, Hermanville, Lion, Hillman, Périers, Biéville, Lebisey and Caen; Axis objectives retaking Pegasus Bridge and Ranville and reaching Hermanville.",
